2026-10-14 - Version 6.0

This major release adds some new features and fix issues reported by users
since last release.

  - Restrict use of pg_password_history to pg_read_all_stats and add missing
    entries in the 5.1.0 to 6.0.0 update SQL file.  Thanks to Georgy Shelkovy
    for the report.

  - Allow integration of the max_auth_failure feature with pgbouncer.
    See https://github.com/HexaCluster/credcheck#using-max-auth-failure-with-pgpouncer
    for explanation. Thanks to sefabalica for the feature request.

  - Fix pgbouncer failure when password_change_first_login is enabled.
    When a role must change its password first, we only want to allow the
    password change itself (ALTER ROLE, handled by the caller) plus the handful
    of harmless session/transaction-control statements that clients and
    connection poolers unavoidably issue while establishing a session.
    Thanks to Cihan Baran for the report.

  - Add credcheck.password_valid_min setting to force a short VALID UNTIL
    window on newly created roles (CREATE ROLE) while keeping the longer
    credcheck.password_valid_until window when existing roles change their
    password. Useful to force new users to change their password quickly
    when the client (DBeaver, pgAdmin, ...) does not support
    credcheck.password_change_first_login. Thanks to remib-x for the
    feature request

 -  Replace the dynahash-backed pg_password_history with an integer indexed
    hash table living entirely in a mmap file.
    Backward compatibility: v5.x dump file are loaded into memory BEFORE the
    mapping is initialised, re-inserts the entries into the fresh store and
    renames the old file to pg_password_history.legacy-migrated so the migration
    runs once. It runs identically on a standby, so the 5.x -> 6.0 upgrade restart
    preserves the history on both nodes.
    
  - Add an optional last-login history modelled on the Unix last(1)/wtmp.
    Records user sessions (name, login time, duration, backend pid, source
    IP and port, and optionally the last SQL query) plus PostgreSQL
    boot/shutdown/crash markers.
    New GUCs: credcheck.lastlog, credcheck.lastlog_max,
    credcheck.lastlog_query_size, credcheck.lastlog_track_query,
    credcheck.lastlog_flush_interval. New view pg_lastlog over
    credcheck_lastlog().

  - Change the behavior of the credcheck.no_password_logging to not hide the
    error statement anymore but instead to replace every PASSWORD '<literal>'
    clause with dots inplace of the literal.
    Now when credcheck.no_password_logging is enabled (the default),
    credcheck masks the value of every PASSWORD '<literal>' clause (and the
    FDW/user-mapping "password '<literal>'" option) wherever it can reach the
    log.

  - Add function pg_check_password( IN username name, IN password text ) to
    check a candidate password (and the associated username) against the
    credcheck username/password policy without creating or altering a role.
    Raises the same error as CREATE/ALTER ROLE would when the policy is violated,
    and returns true when the password is accepted.
    Note: the password reuse policy is not evaluated here

  - Use palloc for transient strings to fix memory leaks and a heap
    overflow.

  - Remove test with SHOW password_encryption for PG13 compatibility

  - Change CI workflow to allow local connections on unix socket for test 08

  - Add select test after force change password.

  - Fix privilege escalation via self ALTER ROLE when password_valid_until is
    set. Restrict the elevation to a pure password change on the caller's own
    role: the statement is only run with superuser privilege when its sole
    option is PASSWORD (plus the VALID UNTIL clause credcheck injects
    itself). Any other role option makes credcheck skip the elevation and
    let core enforce its normal permission checks, which reject the attempt.
    Thanks to Alexander Kukushkin for the patch.

  - Replace use of strtok with strtok_r. Thanks to pavagin for the patch.
    
  - Depending on the current month, time, and time zone, 60 days in the
    future is sometimes displayed as "2 mons" or "1 mon 30 days" or possibly
    something else. Relax to checking between 59 and 61 days ahead.
    Thanks to Christoph Berg for the patch.    

  - Document that password history is replication-aware on PG 15+.
    Thanks to Aidar Imamov for the patch.

  - Remove redundant PG_MAJORVERSION variable. Thanks to Aidar Imamov for the patch.

  - Since the introduction of the custom WAL resource manager, every
    mutation of the password history file is shipped to standbys through
    normal WAL streaming.  Mention this in the 'Password reuse policy'
    section so users are aware that the history stays consistent across
    a primary/replica setup and survives a failover.

  - Fix off-by-one buffer overflow in to_nlower(). Thanks to Aidar Imamov
    for the patch.


